\subsection{Run KTH's SAML procedure}

On the first stop in the sequence (contained in [[response]]), there is a form 
that must be posted.
A browser uses JavaScript to do this, we must do it manually:
Find the form, fetch all values from the input tags, then post the form.
All in all, we want to produce a new response from the server, 
[[saml_response]] from above.

We start by extracting any form.
If there is no form, there is nothing for us to forward.
<<run SAML procedure, produce new response>>=
logger.debug(
  "SAMLlogin starting for %s",
  weblogin._sanitize_url(response.url),
)
doc_tree = html.fromstring(response.text)
try:
  form = doc_tree.xpath("//form")[0]
except IndexError:
  <<don't handle this request>>
else:
  <<extract form data and action>>
  <<post form action>>
@

A page without a form is either the end of the provider's part or an error.
An error page means that the login failed, and we say so, rather than
returning a page that the caller would take for the service's answer.
Otherwise we leave the page as it is: it becomes our response, and we have
forwarded nothing.
<<don't handle this request>>=
logger.debug(
  "SAMLlogin found no form in %s",
  weblogin._sanitize_url(response.url),
)
if response.status_code != requests.codes.ok:
  raise weblogin.AuthenticationError(
    f"SAML error: status {response.status_code} and no form at "
    f"{weblogin._sanitize_url(response.url)}")
saml_response = response
@

To extract the data, we simply traverse the inputs available in the form.
<<extract form data and action>>=
data = {}
for variable in form.xpath("//input"):
  if variable.name:
    data[variable.name] = variable.value or ""

action_url = urllib.parse.urljoin(response.url, form.action)
@

Now here is the trick:
The form data extraction and submission above
([[<<extract form data and action>>]]) is so general, that it can handle each 
of the several steps.
However, we can't pretend that we are done and set
[[self.__logging_in = False]], this will result in infinite recursion.
(The requests will be intercepted before they happen, so we will never 
proceed.)
But we can recursively call this handler again and again until done.
<<post form action>>=
logger.debug(
  "SAMLlogin forwarding form to %s",
  weblogin._sanitize_url(action_url),
)
saml_response = session.request(form.method, action_url, data=data)
forwarded = True

if saml_response.status_code != requests.codes.ok:
  raise weblogin.AuthenticationError(
                      f"SAML error: not OK response: {saml_response.text}")
@

The SAML handler uses the same re-entrancy guard as the other handlers, so it
must clear that guard even when forwarding the SAML form raises an exception.
<<test functions>>=
def test_SAMLlogin_resets_logging_in_after_exception(monkeypatch):
  handler = weblogin.kth.SAMLlogin()
  session = weblogin.AutologinSession([handler])

  response = requests.Response()
  response.status_code = requests.codes.ok
  response.url = "https://saml-5.sys.kth.se/idp/profile/SAML2/Redirect/SSO"
  response._content = b"""<html><body>
    <form method='post' action='next'>
      <input name='token' value='value'>
    </form>
  </body></html>"""
  response.history = []

  def fail_request(*args, **kwargs):
    raise RuntimeError("boom")

  monkeypatch.setattr(session, "request", fail_request)

  try:
    handler.login(session, response)
  except RuntimeError:
    pass
  else:
    assert False

  assert handler.need_login(response)
@

\subsection{Re-running the triggering request}\label{SAMLrerun}

With [[rerun_requests]], [[SAMLlogin]] repeats the request that led to the
identity provider once the relay is done.
That request is usually the caller's own, for instance a SharePoint API call
that was sent to KTH's identity provider for want of a session.
Once the relay has given the service its session cookie, the repeated request
gets the answer that the caller wanted.

But the triggering request is not always the caller's.
Handlers share a session, and another handler's login can pass through KTH's
identity provider too.
LADOK's [[SSOlogin]] (\cref{LogIntoLADOK}) posts the SAML response from the
home organization to the provider, as one step of its own flow.
The provider redirects to a relay form, which [[SAMLlogin]] claims and
forwards to LADOK.
Then the triggering request is that post: a protocol message that the
provider accepts once.
Repeating it gave an error page, which [[SAMLlogin]] claimed again, found no
form in, and repeated the post once more.
That went on until Python's recursion limit stopped it; with [[ladok3]]'s
retries on server errors, after about a quarter of an hour.
The login itself had succeeded before the first repeat.

Hence three rules for the repeat:
\begin{enumerate}
\item We never repeat a request to the identity provider itself.
  Such a request is a step of some login flow, and the flow that sent it
  continues from the response that we return:
  [[SSOlogin]] finds itself at LADOK and is done.
\item We repeat only after we forwarded a form.
  If there was nothing to forward, nothing has changed, and the repeat would
  get the same response.
\item We repeat at most once.
  If the service sends the repeated request back to the identity provider, we
  relay that round too, but another repeat would only take the same round
  again.
\end{enumerate}
<<the triggering request should be re-run>>=
self.__rerun_requests and args and forwarded
and not self.__rerunning
and not self._is_saml_url(self._request_url(args, kwargs))
@

The handler gets the triggering request as the arguments of
[[requests.Session.request]], where the URL may come positionally or by
keyword.
<<SAML handler helpers>>=
@staticmethod
def _request_url(args, kwargs):
  """Return the URL of a request given as `Session.request` arguments."""
  if args and len(args) > 1:
    return args[1]
  return (kwargs or {}).get("url")
@

The third rule needs to know whether we are inside our own repeat.
The repeat's response passes through [[need_login]] and possibly [[login]]
again, so we keep a flag on the handler for as long as the repeat runs.
We set the flag's default on the class rather than in the constructor:
[[ladok3]] caches its session with [[pickle]], and a handler pickled by an
earlier version is unpickled without running the constructor, so it would
lack an attribute that only the constructor sets.

When the repeat is over we delete the flag instead of setting it to false.
The handler's equality compares the attributes of the instance
(\cref{HandlerEquality}), and a program that shares a session adds its
handlers only if no equal one is present.
After the deletion the class default shows through again and the instance
has the attributes it had before the repeat, so it still equals a fresh
[[SAMLlogin]] with the same configuration.
A flag left on the instance would make every such program add a copy.
<<SAML handler class attributes>>=
__rerunning = False
<<re-run the triggering request once>>=
logger.debug(
  "SAMLlogin re-running %s",
  weblogin._sanitize_url(self._request_url(args, kwargs)),
)
self.__rerunning = True
try:
  return session.request(*args, **(kwargs or {}))
finally:
  del self.__rerunning
@

We cannot record these flows: they pass through KTH's identity provider with
one-time SAML messages, and the failure we guard against needs the provider to
refuse a replay.
So we serve the three parties from memory, through a [[requests]] transport
adapter, and let the real [[AutologinSession]] and [[SAMLlogin]] drive them.
Each route maps a method and a URL (without its query) to a function that
returns the status, the headers and the body.
The adapter keeps every request it serves, so a test can count them.
<<test functions>>=
IDP = "https://saml-5.sys.kth.se/idp/profile"
IDP_POST_SSO = f"{IDP}/Authn/SAML2/POST/SSO"
IDP_REDIRECT_SSO = f"{IDP}/SAML2/Redirect/SSO"
LADOK = "https://start.test.ladok.se"
SP = "https://sp.example.org"

def relay_form(action):
  return ("<html><body><form method='post' action='" + action + "'>"
          "<input name='SAMLResponse' value='assertion'>"
          "</form></body></html>")

class FakeServices(requests.adapters.BaseAdapter):
  """Serves fixed routes from memory and keeps the requests it served."""
  def __init__(self, routes):
    super().__init__()
    self.routes = routes
    self.served = []

  def send(self, request, **kwargs):
    url = request.url.split("?")[0]
    self.served.append((request.method, url))
    status, headers, body = self.routes[(request.method, url)](request)
    response = requests.Response()
    response.status_code = status
    response.headers = requests.structures.CaseInsensitiveDict(headers)
    response._content = body.encode("utf-8")
    response.encoding = "utf-8"
    response.url = request.url
    response.request = request
    return response

  def close(self):
    pass

  def count(self, method, url):
    return self.served.count((method, url))

def session_with(services, *handlers):
  session = weblogin.AutologinSession(list(handlers))
  for prefix in (IDP, LADOK, SP):
    session.mount(prefix, services)
  return session
@

First the case that hung [[ladok3]].
LADOK's [[SSOlogin]] posts the SAML response from the user's home
organization to KTH's identity provider, as one step of its own flow.
The provider redirects to a relay form, which [[SAMLlogin]] forwards to LADOK.
The provider accepts each SAML response once, so a second post of the same
one gets an error page.
The step must reach the provider exactly once and end logged in at LADOK.
<<test functions>>=
def test_SAMLlogin_does_not_replay_a_step_of_the_SAML_protocol():
  posts_to_idp = []

  def idp_post(request):
    posts_to_idp.append(request.body)
    if len(posts_to_idp) > 1:
      return 500, {}, "<html><body>Stale request</body></html>"
    return 302, {"Location": IDP_REDIRECT_SSO + "?execution=e1s1"}, ""

  services = FakeServices({
    ("POST", IDP_POST_SSO): idp_post,
    ("GET", IDP_REDIRECT_SSO):
      lambda r: (200, {}, relay_form(f"{LADOK}/Shibboleth.sso/SAML2/POST")),
    ("POST", f"{LADOK}/Shibboleth.sso/SAML2/POST"):
      lambda r: (302, {"Location": f"{LADOK}/gui/app/gui/"}, ""),
    ("GET", f"{LADOK}/gui/app/gui/"): lambda r: (200, {}, "LADOK"),
  })
  session = session_with(
    services, weblogin.kth.SAMLlogin(rerun_requests=True))

  response = session.request("POST", IDP_POST_SSO,
                             data={"SAMLResponse": "from-adfs"})

  assert response.url == f"{LADOK}/gui/app/gui/"
  assert len(posts_to_idp) == 1
@

When the provider answers with an error page and no form, there is nothing to
